From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: appxpackaging, appxdeploymentclient: bundles whose packages are signed on their own

Newer bundles -- Microsoft's App Installer, Store downloads -- map only
their own metadata in the bundle's block map and sign each payload
package on its own; they also carry stub packages (IsStub="true", under
AppxMetadata\Stub) and AppxMetadata\CodeIntegrity.cat. The reader refused
them ("not in the block map").

- appxpackaging: a bundle's package that the block map leaves out is
  accepted only if the bundle manifest names it (a payload or a stub
  package), at the size it gives, and it contains a signature. Stubs are
  not payload. CodeIntegrity.cat is covered by the signature itself (the
  AXCI record), like the block map.
- appxdeploymentclient: a payload package with a signature of its own is
  copied out and must be trusted (WinVerifyTrust) before it is read, and
  every payload package must be the bundle's (same name and publisher).

With these and 0209, Microsoft's App Installer bundle is verified and read,
and refused only for its missing framework (Microsoft.WindowsAppRuntime.1.8),
as Windows would.

